Q: Why does Rust disallow mutable aliasing? A: <p>Mutable aliasing leads to subtle bugs, including memory safety violations. This is most noticeable in multithreaded code, but it can happen in single threaded code too.</p>
<p>Consider for example the following C++ code.</p>
<pre><code>void appendmultiple(String &amp;foo, string_view bar, int num) {
    for (int i=0;i&lt;n;i++) {
        foo += bar;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Now consider someone writes.</p>
<pre><code>String baz = &quot;hello there&quot;;
appendmultiple(baz,baz,10);
</code></pre>
<p>Oh dear, we now have a use after free bug! Appending to the string may cause the String to reallocate internally, leaving the string_view as a dangling pointer.</p>
<p>The C++ answer to issues like this is to just tell people to be more careful. The designers of rust have decided that is not an acceptable</p>
